{
  "description": "How every Flaggr evaluator matches targeting rules. A rule with condition groups is decided by its groups (each group ANDs its conditions; groups combine with groupOperator, \"or\" by default; a group without conditions matches nobody). Otherwise its conditions decide (conditionOperator, \"and\" by default), and a rule with neither conditions nor groups matches everyone. A matched rule's rolloutPercentage then admits that share of targeting keys (none without a targeting key), and the first rule that matches serves its value. Each case's flag is evaluated with each context; value, reason and variant (absent = none) must come out as given. Unset fields may come as the data plane encodes them, null or \"\" (conditions, groups, rolloutPercentage, value and schedule null; conditionOperator, groupOperator and variant \"\"), and read as unset; an entry of targeting that is null is no rule. Each case also runs as rule-matching-cases.go-wire.json holds it: the flag as the Go data plane encodes it (/api/flags/export, the SSE configuration events), regenerated by its Go test.",
  "consumers": [
    "__tests__/unit/evaluator-parity.test.ts (src/lib/evaluator.ts and @flaggr/evaluator, root Jest)",
    "__tests__/unit/in-process-provider-rules.test.ts (InProcessProvider, root Jest)",
    "packages/evaluator/test/rule-matching.test.ts (@flaggr/evaluator, vitest)",
    "services/flaggr-api/evaluator/rule_matching_parity_test.go (Go data plane; writes rule-matching-cases.go-wire.json)"
  ],
  "cases": [
